"selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492800 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492800 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df557d07-88b6-5980-91f5-1e6a3fe0da71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492803 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5c9d97c-c13f-5d1d-806f-0620d8934268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492804 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6a1b3a9-cf8a-56c9-b44f-3a84bdac3d50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}.$ If $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f1a69d2-da4d-539f-85b3-5e00cfab3ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492828 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(T) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(T) & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation with standard matrix $T$ whose columns are ${v}_{1},$\n ${v}_{2},$ and  ${v}_{3}.$\n\n*If  $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=1$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following, if inserted into the blank space, would lead to a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52e3deff-a33f-5133-be77-3539d124a1dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492858 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{dim}(\\text{span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=1$ this means that $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the invertible matrix theorem the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ is equivalent to $Tx=0.$ Since $T$ is not invertible, the equation has non-trivial solutions.\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement I, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,u,v]^{T}\\,:\\,x-2v=0,\\,u=0\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a90e4a0d-4779-5458-9918-a30c4fa7517b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492891 #edb/ref 1074 "Solving $x-2v=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=2v.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2v \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ 0 \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}+v{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=3.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =5-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,u,v]^{T}\\,:\\,x-2y=0,\\,3u+v=0\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c619e065-6d72-5648-a786-860dc455f368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492911 #edb/ref 1074 "Solving $x-2y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=2y.$ Solving $3u+v=0$ for $v,$ we get $v=-3u.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2y \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ u \\\\ -3u\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}+u{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=3$\n\nII. If $v=[1,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $T$ is invertible.\n\nIII. If there is not $u\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(u)=[1,0,0]^{T},$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e76412ff-223a-5baa-b8c0-73f134709b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492918 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $v$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$, we have that $T(v)=0.$ Therefore $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ thus according to the invertible matrix theorem $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since there is not $u\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(u)=[1,0,0]^{T},$ we have that $T$ is not  a bijection (an invertible transformation). According to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}.$ If $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0e7b715-b8f5-546c-8370-11eb96261ecd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492922 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+0 & =6 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =6-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
